00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.500
And then, of course,
PyTorch is also

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.010
perfectly suited for
production and/or research,

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.607
depending on your needs.

00:04:58.607 --> 00:05:00.440
So now that we've
discussed machine learning

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.720
on Apache Hadoop, let's
discuss Apache Hadoop

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.180
as it pertains to
Google Cloud Platform.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.630
And so our solution to a
managed Apache Hadoop service

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:10.080
is Cloud Dataproc.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.690
And so what is Cloud Dataproc?

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:16.160
It contains managed clusters for
Apache Hadoop and Apache Spark.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.110
Cloud Dataproc
allows you to deploy

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:21.170
clusters in 90 seconds or less
on average for very quick up

00:05:21.170 --> 00:05:22.855
times.

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:24.230
When you're using
Cloud Dataproc,

00:05:24.230 --> 00:05:27.020
you only pay for the time
that your queries are running,

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:29.253
also known as compute time.

00:05:29.253 --> 00:05:30.920
It's perfectly suited
to be configurable

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:32.088
with open-source solutions.

00:05:32.088 --> 00:05:33.630
So, of course, we
have Apache Hadoop.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:34.760
We have Apache Spark.

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.450
We have Apache Pig,
Apache Hive, as well as

00:05:38.450 --> 00:05:40.580
what we call optical
components, which

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:44.000
are some of your favorite
open-source data science

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.827
libraries that you can
include with your cluster

00:05:46.827 --> 00:05:47.660
when you spin it up.

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:51.990
So that includes things such
as Anaconda, Jupyter, Zeppelin,

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:52.490
Presto.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:54.032
And there's a whole
long list of them

00:05:54.032 --> 00:05:56.330
that you can find
on our website.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:58.600
And Cloud Dataproc has
seamless integration

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:02.380
with a lot of GCP services,
including BigQuery and Google

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:04.370
Cloud Storage Connector.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:07.865
Cloud Composer also has a
Cloud Dataproc operator,

00:06:07.865 --> 00:06:10.240
which makes spinning your
cluster up and down using Cloud

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:13.880
Composer super easy to do.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.480
And then Cloud Dataproc also
has the auto-scaling feature

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:17.780
that is currently in beta.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.090
And autoscaling's a
really nice feature.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.007
We're going to talk a
little bit more about it

00:06:21.007 --> 00:06:21.890
later in the session.

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:26.200
But the idea behind it is that
as the pressure on your cluster

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.660
increases or decreases,
Cloud Dataproc can actually

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:31.210
add or remove workers without
you having to manually go

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:32.377
in and add them yourself.

00:06:32.377 --> 00:06:34.210
You can do that, of
course, but this feature

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:37.768
allows you to not
have to do that.

00:06:37.768 --> 00:06:39.560
And so we'll just
discuss a little bit more

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.290
about the architecture
behind Dataproc.

00:06:41.290 --> 00:06:43.207
This will be fairly high
level, but we'll just

00:06:43.207 --> 00:06:45.055
give you a little bit
more of an insight.

00:06:45.055 --> 00:06:46.430
So starting on
the left tier, you

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:47.930
can see that we
have your networking

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:50.900
and your IEM layers like
you would for any other GCP

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:54.600
service in our stack.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.800
Next, you have the
Cloud Dataproc API.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:58.550
And so this is really
awesome for actually

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:00.360
interfacing with your cluster.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:03.770
You have the ability to
interact with the cluster also

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:04.740
via the UI.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:07.760
But using the cloud
console commands,

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.200
you can interact
with your cluster.

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:11.900
You also have access
to the jobs API,

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:15.015
which allows you to preset what
type of job you're submitting.

00:07:15.015 --> 00:07:16.640
So, for instance, if
you're submitting,

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:18.432
let's say, a PySpark
job to Cloud Dataproc,

00:07:18.432 --> 00:07:20.420
you can set that when
you submit the command.

00:07:20.420 --> 00:07:22.320
And then, this
way, Cloud Dataproc

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:25.010
will preconfigure the
runtime environment for you.

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:27.410
And then you also have access
to your Hadoop and Spark

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.840
operations via the Dataproc API.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:35.600
So you have access to all of
those tools through the API.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:37.620
And then, of course,
within the cluster itself--

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.760
so you have your Cloud
Dataproc open source image.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:42.260
And so, as I mentioned
earlier, that

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:44.780
can include your Apache
Spark, your Apache Hadoop,

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:46.233
Apache Hive, Apache Pig.

00:07:46.233 --> 00:07:47.900
And then there's the
optional components

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:49.290
that I was mentioning, as well.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:51.350
So here, we have Jupyter,
Zeppelin, and Presto mentioned.

00:07:51.350 --> 00:07:53.400
But there's a whole list of
them, as I mentioned earlier.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:54.500
You can do Anaconda.

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:55.400
You can do RStudio.

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:57.680
There's a whole bunch of them.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:08:00.380
And then, of course, being
a Hadoop cluster, of course,

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:05.387
you can write out to either
HDFS or to Google Cloud Storage.

00:08:05.387 --> 00:08:07.220
So now that we've
discussed machine learning

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:09.620
on Apache Hadoop and
Google Cloud Platform's

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:11.990
solution to having an
Apache Hadoop service,

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:14.270
we're going to talk about
distributed machine learning

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:14.670
right now.

00:08:14.670 --> 00:08:16.520
But I think Gonzalo has
something to say about it.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:17.687
GONZALO GASCA MEZA: Perfect.

00:08:17.687 --> 00:08:18.600
Thanks, Brad.

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:20.810
So what is distributed
machine learning?

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:23.270
Quite important in
the context of Hadoop.

00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:25.700
If you're a machine learning
engineer, a data scientist,

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:28.065
or you've been experimenting
with deep learning models,

00:08:28.065 --> 00:08:29.690
you probably know
when you are training

00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:32.499
large machine learning models,
they take a very long time.

00:08:32.499 --> 00:08:35.370
So some of those may
take a couple of hours.

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:36.390
Some of those take days.

00:08:36.390 --> 00:08:38.929
And they are very large models
that may take even weeks

00:08:38.929 --> 00:08:40.480
to complete training.

00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:44.330
So the first application for
distributed machine learning

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:46.243
is try to reduce
this training time.

00:08:46.243 --> 00:08:48.410
Another application for
distributed machine learning

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:51.210
will be, try to run
experiments in parallel.

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:53.720
So, for example, if you are
doing hyperparameter searching,

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.550
you can run multiple
modes simultaneously

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:58.470
so you can save some time.

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.857
So let's take a look at how
this journey looks like.

00:09:00.857 --> 00:09:02.690
So normally, when you're
still experimenting

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:05.630
with your deep
learning models, you

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:08.300
can use your local computer or
a virtual machine in the clouds.

00:09:08.300 --> 00:09:10.790
And you start with
a multicore CPU.

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:15.210
That might be OK for various
small training models.

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:17.930
But if you really want to
take advantage and reduce

00:09:17.930 --> 00:09:20.690
the training time, you
can use accelerators.

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:24.455
An accelerator could be
a TPU, a GPU, or an FPGA.

00:09:24.455 --> 00:09:26.330
For this talk, I'm only
going to be referring

00:09:26.330 --> 00:09:28.400
to accelerators as GPUs.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:29.350
So what do you do?

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:31.260
You install the drivers.

00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:32.640
You attach your device.

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:36.500
And if you're using frameworks
like TensorFlow or PyTorch,

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:39.320
you need very little
or no code modification

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:41.360
to start training your models.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:45.870
If you can attach one GPU,
why not to attach more GPUs?

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:47.840
Again, you attach more GPUs.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:49.370
You start doing your training.

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:51.452
And if you're using a
framework like TensorFlow,

00:09:51.452 --> 00:09:52.910
there's something
new in TensorFlow

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:55.020
which is called
distribution strategies.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:59.300
So with distribution strategies,
the goal of the TensorFlow API

00:09:59.300 --> 00:10:02.210
is like, don't care too
much about the underlying

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:03.210
infrastructure.

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.880
So you just define what is
your underlying architecture.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.880
So for example, if you're
running a single node,

00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:11.540
you might use define,
oh, I have two GPUs.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.683
So you can continue adding
GPUs into your single node.

00:10:14.683 --> 00:10:16.850
But at some point, you're
going to hit some hardware

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:18.200
limitations.

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.050
And from an architectural
perspective,

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:24.560
instead of scaling up, you
want to scale horizontally.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:27.270
So the next obvious
step is going

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:30.890
multi-node, multiple GPUs.

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:33.530
Well, multiple nodes
and multiple GPUs

00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:36.080
might help you reduce
your training time.

00:10:36.080 --> 00:10:39.150
In reality, you need to
consider other factors.

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:41.210
For example, how is
the communication

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:43.970
between the devices
and network overhead

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:45.440
between those multiple nodes?

00:10:48.665 --> 00:10:50.790
So when we talk about
distributed machine learning,

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:52.710
there are two main
paradigms that we always

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:54.280
need to talk about.

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:58.090
So the first paradigm is
called data parallelism.

00:10:58.090 --> 00:11:00.960
So with data
parallelism, you normally

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:04.387
have your neural network or your
model and you store it locally.

00:11:04.387 --> 00:11:05.970
And then you start
training your model

00:11:05.970 --> 00:11:08.380
with different slices of data.

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:11.118
So this could be
synchronous or asynchronous.

00:11:11.118 --> 00:11:13.410
If it's asynchronous, which
is like the traditional way

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:15.450
of doing TensorFlow,
you normally

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:17.940
train your model
different slices of data.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.610
And the workers might be
doing the heavy lifting.

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:24.180
For example, they will be
computing all the gradients.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:27.000
And your parameter
server or another server

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:30.510
might be just doing the
averaging of those gradients.

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:32.280
That would be like
the asynchronous, way.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:34.920
And there are new techniques,
like new distribution

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:35.710
strategies.

00:11:35.710 --> 00:11:37.960
For example, a
mirroring strategy,

00:11:37.960 --> 00:11:39.780
which actually is
called synchronous,

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:43.138
where all the devices compute
the gradients locally.

00:11:43.138 --> 00:11:45.180
And they communicate those
directly or indirectly

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:45.960
to others.

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:50.240
They will be updating the
model only when it's required.

00:11:50.240 --> 00:11:52.050
But sometimes, your
model is quite large,

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:55.030
that you actually cannot
feed it into one device.

00:11:55.030 --> 00:11:57.030
So if you think of
a neural network,

00:11:57.030 --> 00:11:59.370
you might need to split
these different layers

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:01.390
into multiple devices.

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:02.850
So TensorFlow allows
you to execute

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:06.490
different parts of the
graph in different devices.

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:08.200
This is called
model parallelism.

00:12:08.200 --> 00:12:10.290
So an example of
these models would be

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.700
wide and deep neural networks.

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:16.000
And one example is the Google
neural machine translation

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:16.500
model.

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:17.790
That's a very large model.

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:24.640
So this slide is taken from
the TensorFlow web page.

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:27.570
This is a training model
that the TensorFlow team did.

00:12:27.570 --> 00:12:29.490
So basically, what
they did was, they

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:31.620
collected four different
models which are

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:33.270
very popular for benchmarking--

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:37.200
InceptionV3, VGG16,
ResNet-50 and 152.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.180
And they trained them
with different GPUs--

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.180
one, two, four, and eight.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:43.380
So what they actually
found is, the more

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.810
GPUs that we are
actually add, the faster

00:12:45.810 --> 00:12:47.710
we can process those images.

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:49.560
So you can see there's
a linear growth based

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:51.922
on the number of
GPUs that we add.

00:12:51.922 --> 00:12:54.380
And the faster we're actually
able to process those images,

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:58.780
the faster the training
is actually happening.

00:12:58.780 --> 00:13:03.070
But what happens when
you go from research

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:05.390
or experimentation
to production?

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:06.820
There's more than training.

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:08.580
So you normally have a pipeline.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:11.650
And I'm going to go through
a very simplified, probably

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:14.770
oversimplified machine
learning pipeline.

00:13:14.770 --> 00:13:16.930
And you're going to start
with data preparation.

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:20.590
So think about feature
engineering or your regular ETL

00:13:20.590 --> 00:13:23.680
process where you are
collecting your data,

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:26.100
you are selecting which
features you need.

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:29.110
And normally, in this
case, you don't need a GPU.

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:31.630
Probably, you are fine
with a CPU cluster.

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:33.310
If you think of the
Apache ecosystem,

00:13:33.310 --> 00:13:36.460
you will be using Apache
Spark on this one.

00:13:36.460 --> 00:13:41.620
Once you collect your data
and it's ready to be trained,

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:43.090
you have your
trained data now, you

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:46.720
move to a different stage,
which is the training data.

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:52.720
So we just agree that using
accelerators is a good option.

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:55.270
And you have access
to those devices.

00:13:55.270 --> 00:13:58.210
In reality, if you work
with different departments

00:13:58.210 --> 00:14:01.340
or you are running different
experiments in your company,

00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:03.910
you probably need to be
considerate about how

00:14:03.910 --> 00:14:06.100
you use these devices.

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:09.790
When you allocate a GPU, when
you allocate an accelerator,

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:12.100
you need to make sure that--

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:13.780
you need a resource manager.

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:17.170
It's not like I'm just going
to be configuring my TensorFlow

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:20.500
web page, my TensorFlow
work, and it's just

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:23.240
going to be working.

00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:28.630
So once you move from training,
you go to the serving phase

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:32.420
where you can have
both CPU and GPUs.

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:35.080
So if you think of
serving, you would

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:37.870
use Kubernetes, maybe, some
Docker containers running

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:39.290
TensorFlow serving.

00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:40.940
And you will be fine,
maybe, with CPU.

00:14:40.940 --> 00:14:42.357
But if you really
want to increase

00:14:42.357 --> 00:14:47.770
the speed for your inference,
you may want to add GPUs.

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:51.100
A good example of a GPU that
is designed only for inference

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:53.200
would be the Tesla
T4 from NVIDIA.

00:14:55.890 --> 00:15:00.963
So we agreed that when
you use resources,

00:15:00.963 --> 00:15:02.130
you need a resource manager.

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:05.100
And there's already applications
that solve this problem.

00:15:05.100 --> 00:15:09.420
Think about YARN, think
about Mesos or Kubernetes.

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:10.890
But how do you
configure, actually,

00:15:10.890 --> 00:15:15.220
TensorFlow to have
access to these devices?

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:17.760
In the classical way for
configuring TensorFlow,

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:19.560
you normally define
your cluster.

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:21.900
And you define the IP
address to some ports,

00:15:21.900 --> 00:15:24.670
which are going to be
running your TensorFlow code.

00:15:24.670 --> 00:15:26.920
You might define your workers,
your parameter servers.

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:30.960
Then you stop and you start
your servers based on your job.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:33.090
But this is something data
scientists don't really

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:37.780
want to do, or this is very
focused on dev operations.

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.690
There's something
called tf.config,

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:42.450
which is an environmental
variable that is designed

00:15:42.450 --> 00:15:43.750
to address this problem.

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:49.028
So a lot of a resource
managers, like Kubernetes,

00:15:49.028 --> 00:15:50.570
supports this
environmental variable.

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:53.150
So basically, you define
a cluster specification

00:15:53.150 --> 00:15:54.830
which you're only
going to be reading.

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:57.050
And then from there, you
can execute your code.

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:07.430
So we're going to go through
some of the list of products

00:16:07.430 --> 00:16:10.070
that actually already
address this problem,

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:14.480
meaning resource allocation
and running distributed machine

00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:17.110
learning workflows.

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:19.200
Who is familiar with
some of these products

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:22.740
like Kubeflow, AI Platform,
Horovod, TensorFlow on Spark

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:24.060
or Spark Deep Learning?

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:26.816
Anybody has used any of this?

00:16:26.816 --> 00:16:27.316
Yeah.

00:16:27.316 --> 00:16:29.590
Cool.

00:16:29.590 --> 00:16:31.540
So one of the
things that we care

00:16:31.540 --> 00:16:35.300
in the context of this talk is
that it's supported on YARN.

00:16:35.300 --> 00:16:36.990
It runs on Hadoop.

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:40.140
So the first
product is Kubeflow.

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:42.790
So it's based on the
Kubernetes environment.

00:16:42.790 --> 00:16:44.920
So this might not
be a good option.

00:16:44.920 --> 00:16:46.620
The second one is AI Platform.

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:50.070
It's previously known as
Cloud Machine Learning Engine.

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:51.360
And we just changed the name.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:56.870
And this is great for
model training, inference,

00:16:56.870 --> 00:16:58.410
and model versioning.

00:16:58.410 --> 00:17:00.030
But it is a hosted
solution by Google.

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:02.140
So you really don't care
about infrastructure.

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:04.819
So we just bring your
model and we handle

00:17:04.819 --> 00:17:06.550
the infrastructure for you.

00:17:06.550 --> 00:17:08.170
The other one is Horovod.

00:17:08.170 --> 00:17:09.275
It's more like a library.

00:17:09.275 --> 00:17:11.327
So Horovod's a library by Uber.

00:17:11.327 --> 00:17:13.410
So Horovod, the problem
that we're trying to solve

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:16.800
is running distributed machine
learning on top of TensorFlow

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:21.180
to be able to speed
up the training time.

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:22.680
And it doesn't
really run on Hadoop.

00:17:22.680 --> 00:17:25.020
It's more like a helper.

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:28.140
And then we have two
products, TensorFlow and Spark

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:33.990
from Yahoo, which was one of
the best of the first solutions

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:35.750
trying to address this problem.

00:17:35.750 --> 00:17:39.560
So TensorFlow on Spark runs
on two different layers--

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:41.960
the Spark layer and
then the TensorFlow--

00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:45.150
the TensorFlow layer, the Spark
layer, and the YARN layer.

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:48.900
From this, you rely on the Spark
to do the resource allocation.

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:52.470
And then you need to pass
that request to YARN.

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:56.620
So you need very little
code modification.

00:17:56.620 --> 00:17:58.650
And it works great
for some workflows.

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:01.535
The other one is Spark Deep
Learning from Databricks.

00:18:01.535 --> 00:18:05.940
So it follows the same paradigm.

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:08.700
And it integrates very
good with the ML flow

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:11.610
ecosystem from Databricks.

00:18:11.610 --> 00:18:15.400
But one of the things that we
actually care is GPU support.

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:17.185
So when we think of
GPU supporting Hadoop,

00:18:17.185 --> 00:18:18.350
if we are using--

00:18:18.350 --> 00:18:20.100
there's something that
we need to mention.

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:23.430
So Hadoop version
3.1 is the version

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:27.870
that, in reality,
supports GPU allocation.

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:30.960
This means when
you are attaching

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:33.930
a GPU to your devices,
resource managing Hadoop,

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:36.300
it's somewhere that you
have a GPU connected.

00:18:36.300 --> 00:18:39.570
And before Hadoop 3.1,
there's another workaround,

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:40.950
which is using node labels.

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:45.680
So basically, you tag your
servers that have a GPU

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:48.330
so when you run your
jobs, you basically

00:18:48.330 --> 00:18:52.290
route that job to the service
that have GPU already.

00:18:52.290 --> 00:18:56.140
But it's not native
support for GPU.

00:18:56.140 --> 00:18:58.520
One of the problems
with this is that when

00:18:58.520 --> 00:19:00.643
you're using TensorFlow
in Spark or Spark

00:19:00.643 --> 00:19:02.560
Deep Learning is that
you have a layer on top,

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:03.810
which is the Spark.

00:19:03.810 --> 00:19:08.700
So Spark also requires to be
able to be aware of the GPUs.

00:19:08.700 --> 00:19:11.140
And there's already some
work in the Spark community.

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:14.380
I think the Spark people know
is going to be supporting this.

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:17.350
But there's still a year
open for this issue.

00:19:17.350 --> 00:19:19.350
So that's why it's
important to, if we really

00:19:19.350 --> 00:19:23.700
want to take advantage of the
GPU, have native YARN support.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:29.980
So then in comes TonY.

00:19:29.980 --> 00:19:31.700
What is TonY?

00:19:31.700 --> 00:19:36.100
TonY is a project which
stands for TensorFlow on YARN,

00:19:36.100 --> 00:19:39.060
although it also
supports PyTorch.

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:42.960
So the main functionality
for TonY is orchestrate,

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:47.120
distribute TensorFlow
or PyTorch jobs in YARN.

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:49.540
But it also supports
single jobs.

00:19:49.540 --> 00:19:52.200
So you can bring
your single node job

00:19:52.200 --> 00:19:56.740
and execute it on Hadoop.

00:19:56.740 --> 00:19:59.790
It was developed by LinkedIn
and it was open source back

00:19:59.790 --> 00:20:02.290
last summer.

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:05.070
So what are the important
things about TonY?

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:09.910
So TonY is going to be
supporting first-class jobs

00:20:09.910 --> 00:20:11.590
from running Hadoop.

00:20:11.590 --> 00:20:15.100
This means when you run a
TensorFlow job or PyTorch job,

00:20:15.100 --> 00:20:17.930
it's going to be the same as
you were running on Spark job.

00:20:17.930 --> 00:20:19.690
It just runs on the same level.

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:24.610
If you're really
invested in Hadoop

00:20:24.610 --> 00:20:26.222
and know your
infrastructure but you

00:20:26.222 --> 00:20:28.180
want to start experimenting
with deep learning,

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:31.450
we want you to minify your
infrastructure [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:31.450 --> 00:20:34.330
So your data scientists and
machine learning engineers

00:20:34.330 --> 00:20:36.560
can start using the products.

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:39.460
So TonY allows you
to have a client--

00:20:39.460 --> 00:20:41.710
and we're going to
go more into details.

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:43.690
You're going to have
a client that you're

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:48.110
going to be using to
execute your jobs.

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:50.590
And one of the
features from Tony

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:53.380
is that you can
request resources

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:55.700
in a very fine-grained manner.

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:57.940
This means if you have a
TensorFlow job or PyTorch,

00:20:57.940 --> 00:21:01.250
you can request how much memory,
how many cores are you going

00:21:01.250 --> 00:21:04.540
to be using, and how many GPUs.

00:21:04.540 --> 00:21:07.780
And jobs require very
little parameters.

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:10.000
So normally, when you
execute a Python script,

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:12.430
you have a virtual environment
with all your libraries.

00:21:12.430 --> 00:21:15.070
And you have your Python files.

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:17.720
So what other thing we need?

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:18.970
You need a configuration file.

00:21:18.970 --> 00:21:21.340
So as little as three options.

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:27.600
You can actually execute a TonY
job over other possibilities.

00:21:27.600 --> 00:21:30.840
And if you're running
long training jobs,

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:32.940
you want to make sure
that those jobs actually

00:21:32.940 --> 00:21:34.540
complete successfully.

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:36.330
So when you launch
a TonY job, you

00:21:36.330 --> 00:21:39.720
need to be able to verify that
all your workers are executing

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:40.260
correctly.

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:42.823
And if some of your
containers actually fail,

00:21:42.823 --> 00:21:44.240
you need to be
able to restart it.

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:51.020
So let's take a look at
the TonY architecture.

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:53.270
The first thing, when you
want to run a TensorFlow job

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:57.080
or PyTorch job, that you
need to bring is your code.

00:21:57.080 --> 00:21:58.670
So you bring your code.

00:21:58.670 --> 00:22:00.200
And the second
thing is, you need

00:22:00.200 --> 00:22:01.880
to bring your
virtual environment.

00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:04.940
So the virtual environment is
going to be in a zip format

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:07.073
or compressed.

00:22:07.073 --> 00:22:09.740
And finally, your configuration,
which is the resources that you

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:10.530
actually need.

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:12.920
So this configuration
is an XML file

00:22:12.920 --> 00:22:18.170
that you can define when you
have CPU, how much memory you

00:22:18.170 --> 00:22:22.400
require for each of the workers
and how many GPUs you need.

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:26.830
So those three things are the
first part of running a job.

00:22:26.830 --> 00:22:28.080
Then you need the TonY client.

00:22:28.080 --> 00:22:31.550
TonY client is a jar file
that is compiled using Gravel.

00:22:31.550 --> 00:22:34.070
So TonY is built on Java.

00:22:34.070 --> 00:22:35.720
So when you launch
TonY, when you're

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:40.160
going to be launching a TonY
job, you execute the job.

00:22:40.160 --> 00:22:45.340
And it's going to
contact Hadoop YARN.

00:22:45.340 --> 00:22:47.270
And it's going to
spin up a container.

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:49.263
So this is the application
master container.

00:22:49.263 --> 00:22:50.680
So application
master container is

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:54.130
going to see what you
configure and negotiate

00:22:54.130 --> 00:22:55.700
the resources with YARN.

00:22:55.700 --> 00:22:57.700
So, for example, if you
negotiate three workers,

00:22:57.700 --> 00:22:59.992
it's going to try to allocate
resources for those three

00:22:59.992 --> 00:23:01.300
workers.

00:23:01.300 --> 00:23:05.820
That's the second
element of TonY.

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:07.640
Then you have your
task executors.

00:23:07.640 --> 00:23:09.390
So task executors is
actually your machine

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:11.250
learning code that is executing.

00:23:11.250 --> 00:23:15.220
And those are based on the
resources that you requested.

00:23:15.220 --> 00:23:17.082
So those are YARN containers.

00:23:17.082 --> 00:23:19.540
So task executors is going to
be your machine learning code

00:23:19.540 --> 00:23:21.324
that is actually executing.

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:28.190
So we have talked a lot
about different topics.

00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:29.778
But do you think
it's time for a demo?

00:23:29.778 --> 00:23:30.820
