00:23:44.388 --> 00:23:45.430
GONZALO GASCA MEZA: Sure.

00:23:45.430 --> 00:23:47.945
Creating a Dataproc
cluster is very simple.

00:23:47.945 --> 00:23:49.570
So the first thing
that you need to use

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:52.420
is, you can use the gcloud
command or you can use the UI.

00:23:52.420 --> 00:23:54.260
If you're going to use
the gcloud command,

00:23:54.260 --> 00:23:55.760
you just need to
define your cluster

00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:57.880
name and the number of workers.

00:23:57.880 --> 00:24:01.360
This will be your node
managers in Hadoop.

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:04.120
Then we can take advantage of
a feature called initialization

00:24:04.120 --> 00:24:07.780
action, which are shell scripts
that are going to be executed

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:09.890
during cluster creation time.

00:24:09.890 --> 00:24:13.660
In this case, we have
two, the TonY shell script

00:24:13.660 --> 00:24:15.160
and the GPU drivers.

00:24:15.160 --> 00:24:17.020
The TonY shell script,
what it's going to do

00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:20.290
is, it's going to clone the
code from the GitHub repo.

00:24:20.290 --> 00:24:23.790
And it's going to build
the YARN file for TonY.

00:24:23.790 --> 00:24:25.710
In the official
shell script, we also

00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:28.890
provide two samples,
one with TensorFlow

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:32.010
and one with PyTorch code,
that you can actually use.

00:24:32.010 --> 00:24:34.410
In the second shell
script what we

00:24:34.410 --> 00:24:37.590
