00:30:30.855 --> 00:30:32.980
So let's just talk about
autoscaling really quickly

00:30:32.980 --> 00:30:36.110
and how you can enable
that on the cluster.

00:30:36.110 --> 00:30:38.320
So Gonzalo was showing
you before how here, we

00:30:38.320 --> 00:30:40.888
have the autoscaling policy that
we've called autoscale_tony.

00:30:40.888 --> 00:30:42.430
And what that actually
looks like is,

00:30:42.430 --> 00:30:45.610
it's just a YAML file that
gets uploaded to Google Cloud.

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:48.710
Here, you can see that there's
only two top-level components.

00:30:48.710 --> 00:30:51.770
We have the worker config
and the basic algorithm.

00:30:51.770 --> 00:30:53.380
So we'll just discuss
what those are.

00:30:53.380 --> 00:30:54.910
We'll start with
the worker config.

00:30:54.910 --> 00:30:56.530
You'll see here that we
have two parameters set.

00:30:56.530 --> 00:30:58.780
We have the min instances
and the max instances,

00:30:58.780 --> 00:31:00.610
respectively, set to two and 10.

00:31:00.610 --> 00:31:02.560
What this means is
that as autoscaling

00:31:02.560 --> 00:31:04.580
is adding and removing
workers to the cluster,

00:31:04.580 --> 00:31:06.955
it will always make sure that
the total number of workers

00:31:06.955 --> 00:31:09.100
is within these two
numbers, inclusively.

00:31:09.100 --> 00:31:11.380
So this will never have
less than two workers

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:14.020
and never more than 10.

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:17.260
And how does it actually
figure out how many workers

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:18.080
it should add?

00:31:18.080 --> 00:31:19.840
So it uses the basic
algorithm for that.

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:22.652
And here, we've set
the time to check

00:31:22.652 --> 00:31:25.110
to be two minutes, as we've
denoted by the cooldown period.

00:31:25.110 --> 00:31:29.140
So every two minutes, it will
look at the available memory

00:31:29.140 --> 00:31:30.700
and the pending memory.

00:31:30.700 --> 00:31:34.060
And then it'll calculate how
many workers it should add.

00:31:34.060 --> 00:31:36.962
And then, once it comes
up with that number,

00:31:36.962 --> 00:31:38.920
it will then multiply it
by either the scale up

00:31:38.920 --> 00:31:40.378
or the scale down
factor, depending

00:31:40.378 --> 00:31:42.802
on how many workers it thinks
it should add and remove.

00:31:42.802 --> 00:31:44.260
And so what this
number does is, it

00:31:44.260 --> 00:31:46.120
gives you some more
fine-grained control

00:31:46.120 --> 00:31:48.700
as to how quickly you
may want the cluster

00:31:48.700 --> 00:31:50.073
to scale up or scale down.

00:31:50.073 --> 00:31:51.490
In this case, we've
set these both

00:31:51.490 --> 00:31:53.470
to 1.0, which means
that the cluster will

00:31:53.470 --> 00:31:56.700
attempt to have as near-perfect
memory utilization as possible.

00:31:56.700 --> 00:31:58.700
But in the event that you
don't care about that,

00:31:58.700 --> 00:32:00.880
and if there's a spike
in your pending memory,

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:03.970
you don't necessarily need to
have a bunch of clusters added.

00:32:03.970 --> 00:32:06.550
You can scale this number down
to, let's say, 0.5 or 0.1.

00:32:06.550 --> 00:32:09.670
Just completely dependent on
whatever your use case is.

00:32:09.670 --> 00:32:11.005
So you have the option there.

00:32:11.005 --> 00:32:13.630
And then, last but not least, we
have the graceful decommission

00:32:13.630 --> 00:32:14.410
timeout.

00:32:14.410 --> 00:32:17.710
And what this value does is, if
your cluster determines that it

00:32:17.710 --> 00:32:19.470
should take down
some of the workers,

00:32:19.470 --> 00:32:23.260
it will actually set, in this
case, a 15-minute lag to it

00:32:23.260 --> 00:32:26.802
so that in case there's any
jobs that are currently running,

00:32:26.802 --> 00:32:29.260
you wouldn't want the cluster
to just take the workers down

00:32:29.260 --> 00:32:32.440
and have those jobs
disappear on you.

00:32:32.440 --> 00:32:33.740
So just a small anecdote--

00:32:33.740 --> 00:32:35.740
Gonzalo and I were recently
